// @test: typecheck-pass
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: protocols, conformance, signatures, contravariance, T1073
// @description: The parameter rule stays silent where the types agree under `unify`.
// @spec: 09-protocols.md - Conformance

// =============================================================================
// The TRUE pole of the parameter rule
// =============================================================================
//
// This file exists so that narrowing the rule cannot narrow it to
// nothing, and so that widening it cannot start refusing correct code.
//
// `NonNeg` IS `Int`: a declaration stores its resolved type in the alias
// table, and `unify` relates a refinement to its base in both
// directions.  An implementation naming either one is naming the same
// type, and the check must not speak.
//
// The first repair asked `lookup_type` for that fact and got the
// declaration's own name back — `type_defs` deliberately holds a
// self-referential `Named` to keep the indirection generic aliases
// need.  The exclusion was inert and this file was red.  The authority
// is `resolve_alias`, the table `unify` itself consults.

type NonNeg is Int { it >= 0 };

type Sink is protocol {
    fn take(n: NonNeg) -> Int;
};

type Box is { v: Int };

implement Sink for Box {
    // Accepting the base type is accepting MORE than the protocol
    // promises to pass — the direction contravariance allows.
    fn take(n: Int) -> Int { n }
}

fn main() -> Int { 0 }
